<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Armenia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Yerevan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The independent Republic of Armenia, a landlocked state in the South Caucasus with a global diaspora and the world's oldest national church.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a> and the 1988 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1988-armenian-earthquake">Spitak earthquake</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR</a> voted for independence in a referendum on 21 September 1991 and became sovereign when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved. It took the flag and anthem of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levon-ter-petrosyan">Levon Ter-Petrosyan</a> became its first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Armenia emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Karabakh War</a> in 1994 with Armenian forces controlling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, but under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and in an energy crisis. Presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-kocharyan">Robert Kocharyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serzh-sargsyan">Serzh Sargsyan</a> led a system closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 2018, as a new constitution made Armenia a parliamentary republic, peaceful protests, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2018-armenian-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a>, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan">Nikol Pashinyan</a> to power.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Defeat in the 2020 war and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan's</a> 2023 takeover of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> brought over 100,000 refugees and a cooling of relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Armenia has since pursued a peace settlement with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and closer ties with the West.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/armenia.jpg" alt="Armenia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Stepan Malkhasyants Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Armenia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yerevan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Levon Ter-Petrosyan, Serzh Sargsyan, Nikol Pashinyan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Apostolic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Armenia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Armenia, Hayastan</dd></dl></aside></div>
